,1.6685894619821844,1.658428468274942,1.6479270431657809,1.637084727288003,1.6259011164571233,1.6143758618922524,1.602508670429398,1.5902993047266578,1.5777475834612507,1.56485338151835,1.5516166301716787,1.5380373172558301,1.5241154873302734,1.5098512418350087,1.4952447392378416,1.4802961951732354,1.4650058825727152,1.4493741317867883,1.433401330698352,1.4170879248275623,1.4004344174281307,1.3834413695750258,1.3661094002435534,1.348439186379791,1.330431462962354,1.312087023055465,1.2934067178533253,1.2743914567157395,1.2550422071950016,1.2353599950540108,1.2153459042756058,1.1950010770631025,1.1743267138320228,1.1533240731929988,1.1319944719258477,1.1103392849448013,1.088359945254887,1.066057943899446,1.0434348298987959,1.0204922101800171,0.9972317494978712,0.9736551703468448,0.9497642528643171,0.9255608347248555,0.9010468110256358,0.8762241341629946,0.8510948137001145,0.8256609162258495,0.799924565204698,0.7738879408179244,0.7475532797958548,0.7209228752413355,0.6939990764443869,0.6667842886880451,0.6392809730454265,0.6114916461680159,0.5834188800652003,0.5550653018750671,0.5264335936264793,0.4975264919924646,0.4683467880349181,0.4388973269406576,0.40918100774885086,0.37920078306983945,0.3489596587953868,0.31846069380037917,0.28770699963600693,0.25670174021445874,0.2254481314851585,0.1939494411025807,0.1622089880856694,0.1302301424689166,0.09801632494511012,0.0655710064998082,0.03289770803757245,3.8473413874435795e-16],\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"lines\",\"line\":{\"color\":\"black\",\"width\":2},\"name\":\"f(x) = x sin(x)\"}],\"layout\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"Upper/lower Riemann sums sandwich the integral\"},\"showlegend\":true,\"width\":700,\"height\":450,\"plot_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"paper_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"xaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"x\"},\"showgrid\":true,\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1},\"yaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"f(x)\"},\"showgrid\":true,\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1}}}"
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "display_data"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Convergence rate of the gap\n\nFor a continuous (in fact $C^1$) function on $[a, b]$, the gap $U - L$ shrinks like $O(1/n)$ for the partition with mesh $h = (b-a)/n$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "compute_gap(n) := block([h, U, L, i, sub_pts, vals],\n  h : (b_int - a_int) / n,\n  U : 0.0, L : 0.0,\n  for i : 1 thru n do block([xL, xR, sub_pts, vals],\n    xL : a_int + (i - 1) * h,\n    xR : xL + h,\n    sub_pts : np_to_list(np_linspace(xL, xR, 50)),\n    vals : map(lambda([x], float(f_riem(x))), sub_pts),\n    U : U + lmax(vals) * h,\n    L : L + lmin(vals) * h),\n  U - L)$\n\nn_list : [4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128]$\ngaps : map(compute_gap, n_list)$\nprint(\"n:           \", n_list)$\nprint(\"U - L:       \", gaps)$\nprint(\"ratios n·gap:\", map(lambda([i], n_list[i] * gaps[i]), makelist(i, i, 1, length(n_list))))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 22,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "n:            [4,8,16,32,64,128]\nU - L:\n             [2.737680452014585,1.4269652533342208,0.7134826266671106,\n              0.3571534847781592,0.17863189139379765,0.08932226778759977]\nratios n·gap:\n             [10.95072180805834,11.415722026673766,11.41572202667377,\n              11.428911512901095,11.43244104920305,11.433250276812771]"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The product $n(U - L)$ stays roughly constant — the gap is $O(1/n)$, as expected.\n\n## Fundamental theorem of calculus [S+N]\n\nTwo halves:\n\n- **FTC I**: if $F(x) = \\int_a^x f(t)\\,dt$ and $f$ is continuous, then $F'(x) = f(x)$.\n- **FTC II**: if $F$ is any antiderivative of $f$, then $\\int_a^b f = F(b) - F(a)$.\n\nMaxima can verify symbolically.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "f_ftc(x) := x^3 + 2*x$\nF_anti : integrate(f_ftc(x), x)$\nprint(\"F(x) = ∫ f(x) dx =\", F_anti)$\nprint(\"F'(x) =\", diff(F_anti, x))$\nprint(\"Should equal f(x) = x³ + 2x — yes.\")$\n\na_ftc : 0$ b_ftc : 2$\ndef_int_via_anti : subst(x = b_ftc, F_anti) - subst(x = a_ftc, F_anti)$\ndef_int_direct : integrate(f_ftc(x), x, a_ftc, b_ftc)$\nprint(\"∫₀² f via antiderivative:\", def_int_via_anti)$\nprint(\"∫₀² f directly:          \", def_int_direct)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 23,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "F(x) = ∫ f(x) dx = x^4/4+x^2\nF'(x) = x^3+2*x\nShould equal f(x) = x³ + 2x — yes.\n∫₀² f via antiderivative: 8\n∫₀² f directly:           8"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Numerical: trapezoidal vs Maxima symbolic\n\n`np_trapz` integrates a sampled function with the trapezoidal rule. The error is $O(h^2)$ for $C^2$ functions.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "n_pts : 100$\nxs : np_linspace(0, 2, n_pts)$\nys : ndarray(map(lambda([x], float(f_ftc(x))), np_to_list(xs)))$\ntrapz_val : np_trapz(ys, xs)$\nsym_val : float(integrate(f_ftc(x), x, 0, 2))$\nprint(\"trapz(np_trapz):    \", trapz_val)$\nprint(\"symbolic (Maxima):  \", sym_val)$\nprint(\"difference:         \", sym_val - trapz_val)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 24,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "trapz(np_trapz):     8.000408121620241\nsymbolic (Maxima):   8.0\ndifference:          -4.0812162024117526e-4"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Not Riemann integrable: Dirichlet's function\n\n$D(x) = 1$ if $x \\in \\mathbb{Q}$, else $0$. On any sub-interval $[c, d]$ both rationals and irrationals are dense, so $\\sup D = 1$ and $\\inf D = 0$. Upper sum = $b - a$, lower sum = $0$ — the gap never closes. So $D$ is **not** Riemann integrable.\n\nLebesgue's theory rescues this — $D = 0$ almost everywhere with respect to Lebesgue measure, so $\\int D\\,d\\mu = 0$. That's a different course.\n\n## What we got\n\n- The integral is squeezed between upper and lower sums; the squeeze closes for continuous functions.\n- The fundamental theorem turns integration into the inverse of differentiation.\n- For functions Maxima can integrate symbolically, the symbolic answer is exact; for those it can't, numerical methods (trapz, Simpson, Gauss) deliver $O(h^p)$ approximations.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}